WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.670
They say that learning Russian is very difficult. I already spoke a number of languages

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:12.799
when I decided to learn this language, because I wanted to learn a more difficult and exotic language. It was a

00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:18.320
fantastic decision, which literally changed my life.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.519
If you are thinking of learning russian,

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:29.490
get this: learning Russian brings about a lot advantages.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:35.250
You are not only going to be able to communicate with millions of people,

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:42.360
but the structure of your brain itself is going to change and improve, since you have to think

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.469
in a different way by using new and unusual linguistic structures.

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:55.550
Now, let me tell you how I have learned Russian myself.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:04.630
The first lesson I have learned is that especially with languages

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:11.280
which do not have a latin alphabet, it is quite important to know

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.270
how to start on the right foot. Cyrillic is not very difficult per se,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:23.320
you can learn it in a few days, no problems pop up when you have to

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:29.290
type on the keyboard. The thing is, the Russian keyboard has a

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:37.240
different disposition of letters, and you have to get used to a completely different

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:44.710
typing system. It is a bit like learning to play the piano from the start again, but with

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:50.979
different notes. I have downloaded a phonetic keyboard

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:57.729
and THAT keyboard is very easy and intuitive to use. When I started using it, typing in Russian

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:03.150
became simpler and faster.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:10.690
Russian has cases, and native Russian speakers take them

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:19.150
for granted. They don't learn cases from grammar tables, they learn

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:26.560
by actively using the language. Kato Lomb, a famous Hungarian

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.489
polyglot, reminded us of this fact, and it is something very important to take into account when it comes to learning

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:38.980
languages with a (different and) difficult structure. Whichever language you want to learn,

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:44.440
start listening and reading from the very beginning, and only later

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:51.370
use grammar books, and if you do that, everything will be all right. As I said

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.950
at the start of this video, by learning Russian you will reap a lot of benefits, and that

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:03.250
became evident when I landed in Russia for the first time. At first glance,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:09.330
you can get the impression that Russians are a bit cold, but if you speak

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:14.810
in their language, they become quite friendly and warm.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:21.739
But let's go back in time. I have always been passionate

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:27.829
about the history of World War II. From the get-go, I was fantasizing about speaking with those

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:33.500
who lived this challenging period for the Russian people. Although

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:39.680
I could not, unfortunately, meet those people, thanks to the Russian language, I was able to read

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.840
books and articles (as well as watch movies) and this enormously helped me understand

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:52.220
history from the point of view of Russian. Russian has broadened my vision of

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:58.340
space, time and language, and I am proud of being able to tell you all this

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.840
in the Russian language. Thanks a bunch for listening to me

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:09.349
and if you want to know more, click on the link in the description box, I have written an article

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:17.049
in English about how I have learned Russian on my blog. Bye bye!

